// ================================================================================================
// PIN POSTERIOR -- the Easley-Kiefer-O'Hara-Paperman (1996) informed-trading mixture, estimated by
// Yan (2009, SSRN 1361921), traded as an intraday probability.
//
// READ THIS BEFORE THE INPUTS. THIS RULE DOES NOT CLEAR ITS OWN CONTROL AND IT DOES NOT REACH THE
// PROFIT FACTOR IT WAS ASKED FOR. It is shipped because it was asked for, with every measured
// number attached, so the evidence travels with the code. No edge is claimed.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE MODEL SAYS, AND WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRADEABLE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EKOP model each day as a mixture. An information event occurs with probability a and is bad with
// probability d. Informed traders then arrive at rate mu on ONE side; uninformed traders arrive at
// rates eps_b and eps_s whether or not there is news -- liquidity, rebalancing, market-making
// obligation. PIN = a*mu / (a*mu + eps_b + eps_s) is the fraction of trades that are informed.
//
// PIN ITSELF IS NOT A SIGNAL. It is unconditional and non-directional -- a cross-sectional
// asset-pricing quantity used to explain expected returns over months. It says nothing about which
// way to trade today. What IS directional is the POSTERIOR from the same mixture given the flow
// observed SO FAR TODAY, which is the market maker's own belief update:
//
//   P(good | B,S)  prop  a(1-d) * Pois(B; eps_b+mu) * Pois(S; eps_s)
//   P(bad  | B,S)  prop  a*d    * Pois(B; eps_b)    * Pois(S; eps_s+mu)
//   P(none | B,S)  prop  (1-a)  * Pois(B; eps_b)    * Pois(S; eps_s)
//
// PINE HAS NO lgamma AND DOES NOT NEED ONE. The -lgamma(B+1) - lgamma(S+1) terms are IDENTICAL in
// all three hypotheses, so they cancel in the normalisation. Only k*log(lambda) - lambda survives.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE MEASURED RESULT (research/pin/, NQ 10m, 105,639 bars, 765 sessions, MNQ costs, one contract)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Gate 1, volume-weighted B and S, 2.5xATR stop, no target, 1R trail armed at 1R, flat at the close:
//
//   thresh   block      n   /yr    pts/trade    PF     win   Sharpe
//    0.50  research    722   366      +2.63    1.078  48.8%   +0.57
//    0.50  LOCKED      315   296      +4.06    1.076  51.4%   +0.46
//    0.70  research    479   243      +1.29    1.037  51.4%   +0.23
//    0.70  LOCKED      232   218     +15.23    1.287  53.5%   +1.25
//    0.85  research    278   141      -2.82    0.928  46.0%   -0.39
//    0.85  LOCKED      156   147     +23.18    1.427  54.5%   +1.49
//    0.95  research    119    60      +4.17    1.116  51.3%   +0.35
//    0.95  LOCKED       83    78     +32.53    1.606  62.7%   +1.43
//
// AGAINST A MATCHED RANDOM ENTRY -- same window, same rate, same side mix, same exits, 300 draws:
//   research p 0.067 / 0.197 / 0.590 / 0.157 at the four thresholds -- 0 OF 4 CLEAR at p<=0.05.
//   LOCKED   p 0.243 / 0.027 / 0.013 / 0.003 -- THREE OF FOUR CLEAR.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PARITY -- THIS SCRIPT'S OWN ORDER MODEL, NOT THE RESEARCH ENGINE'S (research/pin/pin_parity.py)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The table above is the research engine. This script cannot do three things it does, so its own
// model was written out in Python and run on the same 105,639 bars:
//
//              research engine          this script's model        gap
//   thresh   n    pts     PF        n    pts     PF          n      pts
//    0.50   722  +2.63  1.078      636  +2.97  1.085       0.88   +0.34
//    0.70   479  +1.29  1.037      413  +1.17  1.031       0.86   -0.12
//    0.85   278  -2.82  0.928      243  -2.51  0.938       0.87   +0.31
//    0.95   119  +4.17  1.116      103  +2.00  1.052       0.87   -2.17
//
// The three differences, none of which can be removed:
//   1. THE VOLUME NORMALISER IS CAUSAL HERE. The research divides B and S by the mean volume per
//      in-window minute over the WHOLE sample; a script cannot read that, so this one uses an
//      expanding mean of the volume seen so far and waits 500 sub-bars before trading.
//   2. THE STOP IS ROUNDED TO A TICK, because strategy.exit(loss=) is priced in ticks.
//   3. THE TRAIL ACTS ON A CLOSE AND FILLS AT THE NEXT OPEN. The research walks an intrabar stop
//      level; a script that reprices a stop cannot protect the FILL bar, where
//      strategy.position_avg_price is still `na` -- so the bracket goes out fill-relative with
//      the entry and the trail starts one bar later.
// The script reads WORSE at the two thresholds where it differs most, which is the conservative
// direction. The control p-values above are the research engine's; the per-trade results agree
// within the gap shown, so the verdict is unchanged.
// Every research cell fails and every locked cell passes, at every threshold, systematically. A
// rule chosen on research should look better THERE. That is the wrong shape and it is the most
// uniform instance of it on this branch.
//
// THE PROFIT FACTOR ASKED FOR WAS 2.0. The best research cell is 1.116. At its measured win
// (+78.25 pts) and loss (-73.75) sizes, PF 1.5 needs a win rate of 0.586, PF 2.0 needs 0.653 and
// PF 3.0 needs 0.739, against the 0.513 delivered -- a lift of +7.3, +14.1 and +22.6 points. The
// best honest win-rate lift measured anywhere on this branch is +1 to +5 points. A 2.79M-cell
// intraday sweep on the same data found ZERO configurations at PF >= 2.0 with >= 200 trades a year
// on the research block, where 89.5% of cells are profitable. The ask is arithmetic, not modelling.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THE MECHANISM IS WEAK HERE, WHICH WAS KNOWN BEFORE THE BACKTEST
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PIN in the equity literature runs 0.10-0.20 (EKOP's own 90 stocks average ~0.19). Measured on NQ
// it is 0.004 with bar counts and 0.014 volume-weighted -- an order of magnitude smaller.
//   1. PIN IS A SINGLE-NAME MEASURE. It prices FIRM-SPECIFIC private information. There is no
//      insider on the Nasdaq-100, and the literature's own finding is that PIN FALLS with size and
//      liquidity, so an index future is the weakest possible place to apply it.
//   2. LEE-READY NEEDS QUOTE MIDPOINTS. B and S are supposed to be buyer- and seller-initiated
//      TRADE COUNTS. No feed available here carries quotes or aggressor tags, so B and S are a
//      PROXY: one-minute sub-bars signed by their own direction, optionally weighted by volume.
// The informed-to-uninformed arrival ratio mu/(eps_b+eps_s) is the number to watch and the panel
// prints it: 0.0148 with bar counts, 0.0519 volume-weighted. At the count version the posterior
// CANNOT EXCEED 0.405 and no bar ever reaches 0.5 -- an informed arrival rate that is 1.5% of the
// uninformed one cannot move a Bayesian update off its prior. VOLUME WEIGHTING IS WHAT MAKES THE
// POSTERIOR USABLE AT ALL and it defaults ON for that reason.
//
// WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS WORK: trade-and-quote data with the aggressor side; SINGLE NAMES rather than
// an index, around earnings, M&A or regulatory decisions; and PIN's documented use, which is
// cross-sectional ranking, not intraday timing on one liquid future.
//
// CAUSALITY. Yan's step 1 identifies event days with an event study over the whole period, which is
// two-sided and cannot be traded. Here an event day is one whose session return exceeds k trailing
// standard deviations, and every parameter is fitted on a rolling window of prior sessions that
// CLOSES BEFORE the session it is used in. Nothing reads its own day.
//
// CHART: 10-MINUTE. Sub-bar data is required, so the delta timeframe must be STRICTLY below the
// chart's or request.security_lower_tf returns nothing and no signal can ever fire -- the panel
// says so. Every var write is under barstate.isconfirmed. Research tooling, not financial advice.
// ================================================================================================
//@version=6
strategy("PIN posterior (EKOP / Yan)", "PIN", overlay = false, pyramiding = 0,
     calc_on_every_tick = false, process_orders_on_close = false, initial_capital = 50000,
     default_qty_type = strategy.fixed, default_qty_value = 1,
     commission_type = strategy.commission.cash_per_contract, commission_value = 0.62,
     slippage = 1, max_bars_back = 3000)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- inputs
gM = "The mixture model"
winOpen  = input.int(570, "Session opens (min past NY midnight, 570 = 09:30)", group = gM)
winClose = input.int(960, "Session closes (960 = 16:00)", group = gM)
lookback = input.int(120, "Sessions in the fitting window", minval = 40, maxval = 400, group = gM,
     tooltip = "Every parameter is fitted on this many PRIOR sessions and the window closes before "
     + "the session it is used in. Yan fits on 1.25 years of daily data; 120 sessions is that.")
evK = input.float(1.0, "Event day = |session return| beyond this many trailing sd", minval = 0.25,
     step = 0.25, group = gM,
     tooltip = "Yan's step 1 uses an event study over the WHOLE period, which is two-sided and "
     + "untradeable. This is the causal replacement. Measured alpha at 0.75 / 1.0 / 1.5 / 2.0 sd: "
     + "0.401 / 0.275 / 0.111 / 0.064.")
useVol = input.bool(true, "Weight B and S by volume", group = gM,
     tooltip = "ON BY DEFAULT AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THE POSTERIOR USABLE. EKOP count trades and "
     + "assume size is irrelevant (their footnote 7), but an informed trader with private "
     + "information trades SIZE. Counting sub-bars gives mu/(eps_b+eps_s) = 0.0148, and at that "
     + "ratio the posterior CANNOT EXCEED 0.405 -- no bar ever reaches 0.5, so no threshold above "
     + "that can ever fire. Volume weighting raises the ratio to 0.0519 and the posterior then "
     + "spans the full [0,1].")
ltf = input.timeframe("1", "Sub-bar timeframe for B and S", group = gM,
     tooltip = "Must be STRICTLY below the chart timeframe or request.security_lower_tf returns "
     + "nothing, the counts stay at zero and no signal can ever fire. The panel prints the live "
     + "sub-bar count.")

gE = "Entry"
pThresh = input.float(0.70, "Enter when the posterior reaches", minval = 0.05, maxval = 0.999,
     step = 0.05, group = gE,
     tooltip = "Measured research profit factor by threshold: 0.50 -> 1.078, 0.70 -> 1.037, "
     + "0.85 -> 0.928, 0.95 -> 1.116. NONE of them clears a matched random entry (research p 0.067 "
     + "/ 0.197 / 0.590 / 0.157) and ALL of them look better on the locked block, which is the "
     + "wrong shape. There is no good setting here; 0.70 is the middle of the range measured.")
doLong  = input.bool(true, "Long on P(good news)", group = gE)
doShort = input.bool(true, "Short on P(bad news)", group = gE)

gX = "Exit geometry (as measured -- not optimised)"
stopN = input.float(2.5, "Stop, x ATR14 at the signal bar", minval = 0.25, step = 0.25, group = gX)
trArm = input.float(1.0, "Arm the trail at (R in front, 0 = off)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.25,
     group = gX)
trDist = input.float(1.0, "Trail this many R behind the running high", minval = 0.25, step = 0.25,
     group = gX)
tgtR = input.float(0.0, "Target in R (0 = none)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.5, group = gX,
     tooltip = "No take profit, as measured. On this branch no-target has beaten every target "
     + "tested 25 independent times.")
showHud = input.bool(true, "Show panel", group = "Display")

TZ = "America/New_York"
nyMin = hour(time, TZ) * 60 + minute(time, TZ)
tfMin = timeframe.in_seconds() / 60
inWin = nyMin >= winOpen and nyMin < winClose and dayofweek(time, TZ) != dayofweek.saturday
     and dayofweek(time, TZ) != dayofweek.sunday
lastWin = inWin and nyMin + tfMin >= winClose
newWin = inWin and not inWin[1]
// ta.atr is Wilder's; the research uses ema(tr, n). This matches the research.
atr = ta.ema(ta.tr(true), 14)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- B and S from sub-bars
lc = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, ltf, close)
lo_ = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, ltf, open)
lv = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, ltf, volume)
nSub = array.size(lc)

// THE NORMALISER IS NOT COSMETIC. A Poisson log-likelihood is k*log(lam) - lam, which is NOT
// scale invariant: divide k and lam both by 1,000 and the posterior changes completely. Raw
// contract volume puts B and S in the tens of thousands, where the three hypotheses' log
// likelihoods differ by hundreds and the posterior saturates at 0 or 1 on the first bar of every
// session -- a different strategy, not a rescaled one. The research divides by the mean volume
// per in-window minute, so B and S land on the same footing as the bar COUNTS they replace.
// The research computes that mean over the whole sample; this is the causal form -- an expanding
// mean of the in-window sub-bar volume seen SO FAR -- which converges to it and reads no future.
var float vSum = 0.0
var float vN = 0.0
float vBar = vN >= 500 ? vSum / vN : na

float barB = 0.0
float barS = 0.0
if nSub > 0
    for i = 0 to nSub - 1
        cc = array.get(lc, i)
        oo = array.get(lo_, i)
        vv = array.get(lv, i)
        w = 1.0
        if useVol
            w := na(vBar) or vBar <= 0 ? na : vv / vBar
        if not na(w)
            if cc > oo
                barB += w
            else if cc < oo
                barS += w
    // accumulate AFTER the bar is used, so this bar's own volume never enters its own normaliser
    if barstate.isconfirmed and inWin
        for i = 0 to nSub - 1
            vSum += array.get(lv, i)
            vN += 1.0

// running totals inside the session, and the session's own open for the return
var float cumB = 0.0
var float cumS = 0.0
var float sessOpen = na
if barstate.isconfirmed
    if newWin
        cumB := 0.0
        cumS := 0.0
        sessOpen := open
    if inWin
        cumB += barB
        cumS += barS

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the fitting window
var histB = array.new<float>()
var histS = array.new<float>()
var histR = array.new<float>()
var float pA = na
var float pD = na
var float pEb = na
var float pEs = na
var float pMu = na
var float pPin = na
var int nFit = 0

// [Yan 2009, steps 1-4] alpha and delta from the event labels, eps from non-event days, mu from
// the conditional means in his equations (5) and (6), PIN from equation (8).
fitWindow(bool doFit) =>
    n = doFit ? array.size(histR) : 0
    float a  = na
    float d  = na
    float eb = na
    float es = na
    float mu = na
    if n >= 30
        float sm = 0.0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            sm += array.get(histR, i)
        mean = sm / n
        float ss = 0.0
        for i = 0 to n - 1
            dv = array.get(histR, i) - mean
            ss += dv * dv
        sd = math.sqrt(ss / math.max(n - 1, 1))
        if sd > 0
            int nEv   = 0
            int nBad  = 0
            int nNon  = 0
            int nGood = 0
            float sBn  = 0.0
            float sSn  = 0.0
            float sBgn = 0.0
            float sSbn = 0.0
            int cGn  = 0
            int cBn2 = 0
            for i = 0 to n - 1
                r = array.get(histR, i)
                bv = array.get(histB, i)
                sv = array.get(histS, i)
                ev = math.abs(r - mean) > evK * sd
                if ev
                    nEv += 1
                    if r < 0
                        nBad += 1
                    else
                        nGood += 1
                else
                    nNon += 1
                    sBn += bv
                    sSn += sv
                if (ev and r > 0) or not ev            // good events plus non-events
                    sBgn += bv
                    cGn += 1
                if (ev and r < 0) or not ev            // bad events plus non-events
                    sSbn += sv
                    cBn2 += 1
            if nEv >= 6 and nGood >= 2 and nBad >= 2 and nNon >= 10
                a := nEv / n
                d := nBad / nEv
                eb := sBn / nNon
                es := sSn / nNon
                muB = (sBgn / cGn - eb) / math.max(1.0 - d, 1e-6)
                muS = (sSbn / cBn2 - es) / math.max(d, 1e-6)
                mu := 0.5 * (muB + muS)
    [a, d, eb, es, mu]

doFit = barstate.isconfirmed and newWin and not na(sessOpen[1])
if doFit
    // push the session that just ENDED, then refit -- so the window never contains today
    if not na(cumB[1]) and cumB[1] > 0 and sessOpen[1] > 0
        array.push(histB, cumB[1])
        array.push(histS, cumS[1])
        array.push(histR, (close[1] / sessOpen[1] - 1.0) * 100.0)
        if array.size(histR) > lookback
            array.shift(histB)
            array.shift(histS)
            array.shift(histR)

// The push above is a global-scope statement and runs before this call, so the window is current.
[fA, fD, fEb, fEs, fMu] = fitWindow(doFit)
if doFit
    pA := fA
    pD := fD
    pEb := fEb
    pEs := fEs
    pMu := fMu
    nFit := array.size(histR)
    pPin := na(fMu) or na(fA) ? na : fA * fMu / (fA * fMu + fEb + fEs)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the posterior
// log P(k | lambda) = k*log(lambda) - lambda - lgamma(k+1). The lgamma terms are IDENTICAL across
// the three hypotheses because B and S are the same in all of them, so they cancel in the
// normalisation and Pine never needs a gamma function.
lpk(float k, float lam) =>
    k * math.log(math.max(lam, 1e-9)) - math.max(lam, 1e-9)

float pGood = na
float pBad = na
if inWin and not na(pMu) and pMu > 0 and pEb > 0 and pEs > 0
    frac = math.min(math.max((nyMin + tfMin - winOpen) / math.max(winClose - winOpen, 1), 0.001), 1.0)
    eb = pEb * frac
    es = pEs * frac
    mu = pMu * frac
    lg = math.log(math.max(pA * (1 - pD), 1e-12)) + lpk(cumB, eb + mu) + lpk(cumS, es)
    lb = math.log(math.max(pA * pD, 1e-12)) + lpk(cumB, eb) + lpk(cumS, es + mu)
    ln_ = math.log(math.max(1 - pA, 1e-12)) + lpk(cumB, eb) + lpk(cumS, es)
    m = math.max(lg, math.max(lb, ln_))
    g = math.exp(lg - m)
    b = math.exp(lb - m)
    nn = math.exp(ln_ - m)
    tot = g + b + nn
    pGood := g / tot
    pBad := b / tot

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- orders
var int lastExitBar = -1
var int nClosed = 0
var float riskPts = na
var float peak = na
if strategy.closedtrades != nClosed
    nClosed := strategy.closedtrades
    lastExitBar := bar_index

canEnter = barstate.isconfirmed and strategy.position_size == 0 and bar_index != lastExitBar
     and inWin and not lastWin and not na(atr) and atr > 0
goLong  = canEnter and doLong  and not na(pGood) and pGood >= pThresh
goShort = canEnter and doShort and not na(pBad)  and pBad  >= pThresh and not goLong

if goLong or goShort
    side = goLong ? 1 : -1
    riskPts := stopN * atr
    peak := na
    int ticks = int(math.max(1, math.round(riskPts / syminfo.mintick)))
    int tgtTicks = na            // `na` means no take profit; the type keyword is REQUIRED
    if tgtR > 0
        tgtTicks := math.max(1, int(math.round(tgtR * ticks)))
    if goLong
        strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
        strategy.exit("Lx", from_entry = "L", loss = ticks, profit = tgtTicks)
    else
        strategy.entry("S", strategy.short)
        strategy.exit("Sx", from_entry = "S", loss = ticks, profit = tgtTicks)

// The trail acts on the CLOSE and cannot act on the fill bar: position_avg_price does not exist
// until one bar after the fill, so a level written from it there is `na` and silently does
// nothing. The fill-relative bracket above covers exactly that bar.
sideN = strategy.position_size > 0 ? 1 : -1
if barstate.isconfirmed and strategy.position_size != 0 and bar_index > lastExitBar
     and not na(strategy.position_avg_price) and riskPts > 0 and trArm > 0
    adv = sideN * (close - strategy.position_avg_price) / riskPts
    peak := na(peak) ? adv : math.max(peak, adv)
    if peak >= trArm
        lvl = strategy.position_avg_price + sideN * (peak - trDist) * riskPts
        if (sideN > 0 and close < lvl) or (sideN < 0 and close > lvl)
            strategy.close_all(comment = "trail")

if barstate.isconfirmed and lastWin and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all(comment = "session flat")

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- display
plot(pGood, "P(good news)", color = color.new(#1baf7a, 0), linewidth = 2)
plot(pBad, "P(bad news)", color = color.new(#c0392b, 0), linewidth = 2)
hline(pThresh, "threshold", color = color.new(color.gray, 30), linestyle = hline.style_dashed)
hline(0.5, "even", color = color.new(color.gray, 70), linestyle = hline.style_dotted)
bgcolor(inWin ? color.new(#7a5cd6, 95) : na, title = "session")

var table hud = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 10, border_width = 1)
if showHud and barstate.islast
    sz = size.small
    float ratio = na(pMu) or na(pEb) ? na : pMu / (pEb + pEs)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 0, "PIN posterior (EKOP / Yan)", text_color = color.white,
         bgcolor = color.new(#7a5cd6, 20), text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 0, timeframe.period, text_color = color.white,
         bgcolor = color.new(#7a5cd6, 20), text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 1, "sub-bar delta live?", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 1, nSub > 0 ? str.tostring(nSub) + " sub-bars per chart bar"
         : "NO -- the rule can never fire on this chart", text_size = sz,
         text_color = nSub > 0 ? color.gray : color.new(#c0392b, 0))
    table.cell(hud, 0, 2, "volume normaliser", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 2, not useVol ? "off (bar counts)"
         : na(vBar) ? "warming up -- " + str.tostring(vN, "#") + " / 500 sub-bars"
         : str.tostring(vBar, "#") + " contracts / sub-bar", text_size = sz,
         text_color = useVol and na(vBar) ? color.new(color.orange, 0) : color.gray)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 3, "sessions fitted", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 3, str.tostring(nFit) + " / " + str.tostring(lookback)
         + (na(pMu) ? "  (warming up)" : ""), text_size = sz,
         text_color = na(pMu) ? color.new(color.orange, 0) : color.gray)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 4, "alpha / delta", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 4, na(pA) ? "-" : str.tostring(pA, "#.###") + " / "
         + str.tostring(pD, "#.###"), text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 5, "eps_b / eps_s / mu", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 5, na(pMu) ? "-" : str.tostring(pEb, "#.#") + " / "
         + str.tostring(pEs, "#.#") + " / " + str.tostring(pMu, "#.##"), text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 6, "mu / (eps_b+eps_s)", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 6, na(ratio) ? "-" : str.tostring(ratio, "#.####")
         + (ratio < 0.02 ? "  <- too low, posterior pinned to the prior" : ""), text_size = sz,
         text_color = na(ratio) or ratio < 0.02 ? color.new(color.orange, 0) : color.gray)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 7, "PIN", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 7, na(pPin) ? "-" : str.tostring(pPin, "#.####")
         + "   (literature 0.10-0.20 on single stocks)", text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 8, "P(good) / P(bad) now", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 8, na(pGood) ? "outside the session"
         : str.tostring(pGood, "#.###") + " / " + str.tostring(pBad, "#.###"), text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 9, "MEASURED", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 9, "research PF 1.04-1.12, 0 of 4 clear a random entry", text_size = sz,
         text_color = color.new(#eb6834, 0))
